     WHAT DOES NOT BELONG IN THIS FILE

     These are the real ones, taken from files that had drifted. Each looks
     reasonable in isolation, which is why they accumulate.

     1. A VALUE THAT LIVES IN CONFIG.
        ✗ "Keep .planning/config.json workflow.security_enforcement set to false"
        The config file holds the value. This sentence cannot enforce it, and
        goes stale the moment someone changes the setting. If the REASON
        matters, keep one line of rationale and name the key — never the value.

     2. A RULE THAT IS ALREADY GLOBAL.
        ✗ "Check whether a dev server is already running before starting one"
        ✗ "Stop only background servers you started"
        ✗ "Prefer the browser, then HTTP-only checks"
        All three are in global-prompt.md, loaded in every session already.
        The copy here will drift and then contradict the original.

     3. A RESTATEMENT OF THE MILESTONE RITUAL.
        ✗ "For milestone close, commit the closeout, update the release
           branch, push, then verify the deploy"
        gsd-settings.md §6 owns the sequence. Repos that restate it end up
        with an older version of the ritual that quietly overrides the current
        one. Give the ritual its INPUTS (above) and nothing else.

     4. A RULE THAT CONTRADICTS A GLOBAL RULE WITHOUT SAYING SO.
        ✗ "Think carefully before initiating autonomous production deployments"
        …in a repo whose close ritual is supposed to deploy unattended. If this
        repo genuinely needs a human gate before production, say exactly that,
        say why, and mark it as an exception to gsd-settings.md §6.6. An
        unmarked contradiction just makes the agent pick one at random.

     5. GENERIC GSD OR TOOL GUIDANCE.
        ✗ "Use /gsd-quick for small fixes, /gsd-debug for bugs"
        ✗ "How to pick the next milestone number"
        ✗ "How to name a workstream"
        True everywhere, so it belongs in gsd-settings.md. A copy here is
        maintained by nobody.

     6. HISTORY.
        ✗ Migration research, resolved incidents, superseded decisions.
        Those go in .planning/ or a retrospective. This file is instructions.

     THE TEST, EVERY TIME YOU ADD A LINE:
       Could this be config?              → make it config
       Would it be true in another repo?  → global-prompt.md or gsd-settings.md
       Would it be true for another CLI?  → it is already in the right place
       Does it contradict something above it in the stack? → say so explicitly
